<h2>The allegations</h2>
<p>The informant alleged that the accused persons gathered at her door, abused her and assaulted her family, causing injuries. Within that composite narrative, the role attributed to the petitioner was specific and single: an assault on Ramavadhesh Singh's head. The injury report graded that injury as simple. The petitioner had remained out of the process since 2022, and the State opposed bail on the strength of the assault allegation itself.</p>

<h2>What moved the court</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Role specificity:</strong> one identified act against one injured person, rather than an omnibus allegation spanning the whole occurrence.</li>
  <li><strong>Medical gradation:</strong> the injury was simple in nature — the single most load-bearing fact in assault-case bail practice.</li>
  <li><strong>The deposit:</strong> Rs 10,000 by demand draft with the Nazarat, earmarked for the injured person's medical assistance, expressly without admission of the allegations, releasable to the informant after verification.</li>
  <li><strong>The undertaking:</strong> a commitment to appear diligently at trial and cooperate with the investigation.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The conditions — Patna's standard architecture, with teeth</h2>
<p>The order follows the Patna High Court's now-familiar conditional structure. Release operates if the petitioner is arrested or surrenders within four weeks, on a bond of Rs 10,000 with two sureties of the like amount before the Chief Judicial Magistrate, Bhojpur at Ara. One surety must be a family member or relative, identified by Aadhaar, Voter ID, PAN or driving licence — a verification device that ties the bond to a traceable household. The petitioner must attend every trial date, with two consecutive unexplained absences exposing the bail to cancellation by the trial court itself; he must appear before the police when required during the remaining investigation; he must mark monthly attendance at the police station for six months and file the certificate before the trial court; and the usual prohibitions on witness tampering and fresh offences apply.</p>

<h2>The medical-assistance deposit as a practice device</h2>
<p>The structured deposit deserves attention. Courts are rightly wary of payments that look like purchasing bail; equally, genuine medical assistance to an injured complainant is a legitimate humanitarian gesture that courts may record. The device used here keeps the transaction judicial: the money moves by demand draft, sits with the court's Nazarat, and reaches the informant only after verification. Counsel proposing such a gesture should mirror this structure — through the court, documented, and expressly without prejudice — and should never frame it as consideration for consent or compromise in a non-compoundable matter.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In multi-accused assault FIRs, the petitioner's chart of role attribution versus injury gradation is the core of the anticipatory bail brief: which accused is said to have done what, to whom, and what did the medical report say about each injury. Where the only act attributed to the applicant produced a simple injury, that chart — more than rhetoric about false implication — is what wins the order.</p>
</div>
